1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,577
By the end of this video, you will know how to choose the right wheel size and firmness for the skating you actually do.

2
00:00:07,577 --> 00:00:10,735
You will also learn how to make a set last longer.

3
00:00:10,735 --> 00:00:13,766
Bearings get most of the attention at any track.

4
00:00:13,766 --> 00:00:16,608
Skaters compare ceramics and argue about oil.

5
00:00:16,608 --> 00:00:21,155
But your wheels are the only part of your setup that touches the ground.

6
00:00:21,155 --> 00:00:24,375
They are what turn your effort into forward motion.

7
00:00:24,375 --> 00:00:28,354
Yet most skaters pick them because they were the ones in stock.

8
00:00:28,354 --> 00:00:29,301
Let's fix that.

9
00:00:30,001 --> 00:00:34,748
Wheels are the single biggest performance variable on an inline speed skate.

10
00:00:34,748 --> 00:00:37,684
They decide how much grip you have in a corner.

11
00:00:37,684 --> 00:00:41,806
They decide how much rolling resistance you fight on the straight.

12
00:00:41,806 --> 00:00:44,930
They change how the skate feels over rough tarmac.

13
00:00:44,930 --> 00:00:49,302
And they decide how many kilometres you get before you need a new set.

14
00:00:49,302 --> 00:00:53,612
Get the wheels right, and everything else in your setup works better.

15
00:00:53,612 --> 00:00:57,422
Get them wrong, and even the best bearings will not save you.

16
00:00:57,422 --> 00:01:00,795
So let's start with the first decision, which is size.

17
00:01:01,496 --> 00:01:07,077
Here is a 90 millimetre wheel on the left and a 110 millimetre wheel on the right.

18
00:01:07,077 --> 00:01:08,711
Both meet the same bump.

19
00:01:08,711 --> 00:01:14,973
The bigger wheel meets it at a shallower angle, so it rolls over rough surfaces more easily.

20
00:01:14,973 --> 00:01:18,717
A larger wheel also covers more ground with every turn.

21
00:01:18,717 --> 00:01:26,409
On a smooth track or a good road, a 110 carries speed well and holds it with less effort once you are up to pace.

22
00:01:26,409 --> 00:01:28,314
But look at the axle height.

23
00:01:28,314 --> 00:01:31,309
The bigger wheel sits higher off the ground.

24
00:01:31,309 --> 00:01:37,368
That raises your centre of gravity, and the skate feels less planted and less responsive.

25
00:01:38,068 --> 00:01:39,963
Big wheels have real costs.

26
00:01:39,963 --> 00:01:43,826
They are heavier, so they take more work to accelerate.

27
00:01:43,826 --> 00:01:47,266
They take more work to swing through each stride.

28
00:01:47,266 --> 00:01:50,145
And they need a frame built to take them.

29
00:01:50,145 --> 00:01:55,342
Smaller wheels, at 90 or 100 millimetres, accelerate faster and sit lower.

30
00:01:55,342 --> 00:01:59,204
They give you a more connected, more controllable feel.

31
00:01:59,204 --> 00:02:05,734
On a tight indoor track, with constant corners and constant acceleration, that matters a lot.

32
00:02:05,734 --> 00:02:10,158
Many skaters chase 110 because the top marathon skaters use it.

33
00:02:10,158 --> 00:02:15,144
Then they end up slower on a technical track than they would be on 100.

34
00:02:15,844 --> 00:02:20,708
So ask what you actually skate, not what you hope to skate one day.

35
00:02:20,708 --> 00:02:25,137
Long, smooth, open roads and marathon distances point to 110.

36
00:02:25,137 --> 00:02:33,196
Tight indoor tracks, technical banked circuits and lots of hard acceleration out of corners point to 100 or 90.

37
00:02:33,196 --> 00:02:40,239
Rough road surfaces tend to reward the larger wheel, simply because it survives the bumps better.

38
00:02:40,239 --> 00:02:42,489
And one rule is not negotiable.

39
00:02:42,489 --> 00:02:44,740
Your frame decides the ceiling.

40
00:02:44,740 --> 00:02:50,330
Check the maximum wheel size your frame is rated for before you buy anything.

41
00:02:50,330 --> 00:02:53,017
This catches people out all the time.

42
00:02:53,717 --> 00:02:57,050
The second decision is firmness, called durometer.

43
00:02:57,050 --> 00:03:00,583
It measures how hard the urethane is, on the A scale.

44
00:03:00,583 --> 00:03:05,382
It controls the oldest trade off in skating, which is grip against roll.

45
00:03:05,382 --> 00:03:08,982
A softer wheel deforms more where it meets the ground.

46
00:03:08,982 --> 00:03:11,115
That deformation gives you grip.

47
00:03:11,115 --> 00:03:13,048
It also loses energy as heat.

48
00:03:13,048 --> 00:03:14,914
A firmer wheel deforms less.

49
00:03:14,914 --> 00:03:18,047
It rolls with less resistance and lasts longer.

50
00:03:18,047 --> 00:03:22,514
But it gives you less grip and sends more vibration into your feet.

51
00:03:22,514 --> 00:03:24,780
There is no single correct answer.

52
00:03:24,780 --> 00:03:29,980
There is only the right answer for your surface, your weight and your skating.

53
00:03:30,680 --> 00:03:34,234
Most race wheels come in two firmness options.

54
00:03:34,234 --> 00:03:40,029
In the JUNK range, XFirm is rated 84 to 86A, and XXFirm is rated 86 to 88A.

55
00:03:40,029 --> 00:03:44,898
This scale runs from softer on the left to harder on the right.

56
00:03:44,898 --> 00:03:48,143
You can see the two ranges overlap at 86A.

57
00:03:48,143 --> 00:03:53,320
That is why skaters describe the difference as a shade, not a step.

58
00:03:53,320 --> 00:03:55,329
XFirm holds a little more.

59
00:03:55,329 --> 00:03:57,647
XXFirm rolls a little further.

60
00:03:57,647 --> 00:04:00,275
And notice the line at the bottom.

61
00:04:00,275 --> 00:04:01,975
Heat softens urethane.

62
00:04:01,975 --> 00:04:07,615
So a hot afternoon shifts every wheel towards the soft end of this scale.

63
00:04:08,316 --> 00:04:10,832
Choose XFirm when you want more grip.

64
00:04:10,832 --> 00:04:14,368
That means slick or dusty surfaces, or a cool track.

65
00:04:14,368 --> 00:04:22,189
It also suits lighter skaters, and anyone still developing their corner technique who wants the wheel to help them.

66
00:04:22,189 --> 00:04:27,086
Choose XXFirm when the surface already has good grip, or when it is hot.

67
00:04:27,086 --> 00:04:32,187
It suits heavier or more powerful skaters who can overwhelm a softer wheel.

68
00:04:32,187 --> 00:04:37,695
Temperature matters more than most people admit, especially in Indian conditions.

69
00:04:37,695 --> 00:04:44,768
A wheel that feels perfect on an early winter morning can feel greasy and vague on hot afternoon tarmac.

70
00:04:44,768 --> 00:04:48,305
Skaters who race across seasons often keep two sets.

71
00:04:49,005 --> 00:04:50,111
Now the brands.

72
00:04:50,111 --> 00:04:54,682
Both JUNK and MPC make wheels that world class skaters win on.

73
00:04:54,682 --> 00:04:57,632
One is not simply faster than the other.

74
00:04:57,632 --> 00:05:07,291
The JUNK Voodoo leaves out pigment entirely, so the urethane is exactly what the chemist intended, and it comes in 90, 100 and 110.

75
00:05:07,291 --> 00:05:16,950
The MPC Black Magic uses a softer inner ring inside a harder outer layer, so it flexes for grip while the outer body keeps rolling.

76
00:05:16,950 --> 00:05:20,636
The real difference is feel, and feel is personal.

77
00:05:20,636 --> 00:05:23,807
If you can try a friend's set first, do it.

78
00:05:23,807 --> 00:05:31,106
Remember that sets come as eight wheels without bearings, so plan for sixteen bearings and spacers.

79
00:05:31,806 --> 00:05:33,483
Wheels never wear evenly.

80
00:05:33,483 --> 00:05:38,247
Your inside edges take more punishment, because that is where you push.

81
00:05:38,247 --> 00:05:43,010
Left alone, a wheel cones, with a sharp inside edge and a flat outside.

82
00:05:43,010 --> 00:05:48,244
That gives you unpredictable grip in corners and wastes urethane you paid for.

83
00:05:48,244 --> 00:05:53,410
So swap each wheel to a new position and flip it, so the worn side faces out.

84
00:05:53,410 --> 00:05:57,100
The exact pattern matters less than doing it regularly.

85
00:05:57,100 --> 00:06:01,930
Every couple of weeks is a good start if you train several times a week.

86
00:06:01,930 --> 00:06:06,291
If you can feel a sharp lip with your thumb, you waited too long.

87
00:06:06,291 --> 00:06:11,592
While the wheels are off, check for chunking, cracked hubs and gritty bearings.

88
00:06:12,292 --> 00:06:19,866
To make a set last, keep it clean, store it somewhere cool and out of the sun, and match the compound to your surface.

89
00:06:19,866 --> 00:06:21,535
Here is the short version.

90
00:06:21,535 --> 00:06:25,578
Pick your size for the surface and the skating you actually do.

91
00:06:25,578 --> 00:06:30,007
Pick your firmness for your surface, your weight and the temperature.

92
00:06:30,007 --> 00:06:34,757
Rotate on a schedule, and keep your bearings clean while you are in there.

93
00:06:34,757 --> 00:06:39,892
The full wheel guide, with answers to common questions, is on the LiveLica blog.

94
00:06:39,892 --> 00:06:41,882
The link is in the description.
